<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Calculating the exact perimeter of a square garden from its area in the form k*sqrt(m)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A square garden has an area of \({@x@}\text{ m}^2\). Fencing is needed around the entire perimeter of the garden.</p>
<p>Find the exact length of fencing needed, giving your answer in the form \(k\sqrt{m}\), where \(m\) has no perfect-square factors. <i>(Note: For example, write <code>20*sqrt(13)</code> to represent \(20\sqrt{13}\).)</i></p>
<p>Length of fencing = [[input:ans1]] metres [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Step 1: Find the side length of the square garden</b><br>
The side length of a square is the square root of its area:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \text{Side length} = \sqrt{{@x@}}\text{ metres}\)</p>

<p><b>Step 2: Simplify \(\sqrt{{@x@}}\) by finding the largest perfect-square factor</b><br>
Look for the largest perfect square (\(4, 9, 16, 25, \dots\)) that divides \({@x@}\) evenly. Since \({@x@} = {@a2@} \times {@n@}\) and \({@a2@} = {@a@}^2\) is the largest perfect-square factor of \({@x@}\), split the root using \(\sqrt{p \times q} = \sqrt{p} \times \sqrt{q}\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \sqrt{{@x@}} = \sqrt{{@a2@} \times {@n@}} = \sqrt{{@a2@}} \times \sqrt{{@n@}} = {@a@}\sqrt{{@n@}}\text{ metres}\)</p>

<p><b>Step 3: Calculate the total perimeter of the square</b><br>
A square has \(4\) equal sides, so multiply the side length by \(4\):</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\(\displaystyle \text{Perimeter} = 4 \times {@a@}\sqrt{{@n@}} = {@c@}\sqrt{{@n@}}\text{ metres}\)</p>

<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\(\displaystyle \boxed{ \text{Length of fencing} = {@ta1@}\text{ metres} }\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
